WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.900
I want you to picture a computer program in your mind's eye, like really visualize it.

00:06.140 --> 00:08.080
Most people probably just see matrix-style code, right?

00:08.160 --> 00:14.740
Right, exactly. If you're like most people, you are probably imagining lines of text scrolling

00:14.740 --> 00:20.940
down a screen. You see these rigid brackets, semicolons, you know, strict syntactical rules.

00:20.960 --> 00:22.240
That's very flat.

00:22.460 --> 00:27.980
Totally flat. It's textual. It's, well, fundamentally static. But what if computation

00:27.980 --> 00:30.740
wasn't text at all? Which is a crazy thought.

00:30.800 --> 00:38.060
It is. What if, at its very core, it was geometry? I want you to imagine a universe of floating

00:38.060 --> 00:39.120
nested bubbles.

00:39.160 --> 00:39.940
There's bubbles everywhere.

00:40.080 --> 00:44.700
Yeah, and these bubbles are constantly opening, merging, and popping to actually create reality.

00:44.900 --> 00:50.120
I mean, I know it sounds like a massive abstraction, but this geometric interpretation is actually

00:50.120 --> 00:53.520
the foundation of a really rigorously mathematical framework.

00:53.620 --> 00:55.500
Right, the one developed by the Flushen Research Group.

00:55.500 --> 01:03.320
Exactly. Across a whole series of papers published between late 2025 and mid-2026, they introduced

01:03.320 --> 01:06.180
something called the SpherePop Calculus, or SPC.

01:06.520 --> 01:13.180
SPC, right. And our mission for this deep dive today is to really unpack this, because it

01:13.180 --> 01:15.240
completely rewrites the rules, doesn't it?

01:15.260 --> 01:20.340
Oh, completely. Not just for how we compile code, but, you know, for how we calculate probabilistic

01:20.340 --> 01:26.060
risk. And ultimately, and this is the wild part, how we approach artificial general intelligence

01:26.060 --> 01:26.660
safety.

01:27.020 --> 01:32.460
Yeah, the AGI safety stuff blew my mind. But let's start with the basics. The shift from

01:32.460 --> 01:35.800
syntax to geometry is what caught my attention immediately.

01:35.900 --> 01:37.520
Right, it's a huge paradigm shift.

01:37.680 --> 01:43.280
Because if we think about the lambda calculus, which has dominated functional programming for

01:43.280 --> 01:46.220
decades, scope is defined syntactically.

01:46.220 --> 01:47.840
Right, you literally type it out.

01:47.920 --> 01:53.560
Yeah, you use parentheses or brackets, you enclose a function, and you basically say, everything

01:53.560 --> 01:56.480
inside these brackets belongs to this local environment.

01:56.500 --> 01:57.520
Yeah, it's very rigid.

01:58.000 --> 02:02.760
But Fluxenia just throws out the brackets entirely. They argue that scope should be interpreted

02:02.760 --> 02:06.560
geometrically, like as a physical sphere in the continuous space.

02:06.560 --> 02:13.380
And, you know, that shift is really necessary. Because syntactic enclosure, those rigid brackets,

02:13.840 --> 02:19.600
it fundamentally fails to elegantly capture concurrent probabilistic environments.

02:19.860 --> 02:20.780
Because it gets too messy.

02:20.980 --> 02:28.180
Exactly. In standard functional paradigms, when you have, say, deeply nested asynchronous functions,

02:28.440 --> 02:31.320
all trying to resolve probabilities simultaneously.

02:31.840 --> 02:33.500
Like a dozen things happening at once.

02:33.500 --> 02:40.380
Right. The text-based syntax just becomes this massive bottleneck. SBC replaces that bottleneck

02:40.380 --> 02:42.160
with four geometric primitives.

02:42.240 --> 02:44.160
Okay, four primitives. Let's break those down.

02:44.260 --> 02:49.120
So the first one is the sphere. When you define an abstraction, you are basically blowing a

02:49.120 --> 02:49.820
geometric bubble.

02:49.960 --> 02:50.880
Like blowing a soap bubble.

02:51.100 --> 02:55.680
Yeah. And the interior of that bubble encapsulates the computational state.

02:55.860 --> 02:59.960
Okay, got it. So the first primitive is the sphere. And the second primitive, logically,

02:59.960 --> 03:00.660
is the pop.

03:00.960 --> 03:01.280
Right.

03:01.280 --> 03:04.980
In standard terms, I guess this is application or beta reduction.

03:05.160 --> 03:08.920
Yes, exactly. It's the moment a function actually receives its argument.

03:09.420 --> 03:14.660
But Flyxion treats the pop as a literal physical event, right? The bubble bursts,

03:15.000 --> 03:20.440
it collapses its boundary, and it releases its evaluated state into the surrounding environment.

03:20.940 --> 03:26.620
Yeah, but the transition from a sphere to a pop isn't just instantaneous in the mathematics.

03:26.620 --> 03:27.360
It's not magic.

03:27.360 --> 03:28.880
Okay. How does it work then?

03:29.460 --> 03:34.740
So Flyxion situates this entire calculus inside what they call the RSVP framework.

03:35.160 --> 03:35.680
RSVP.

03:35.740 --> 03:36.020
Okay.

03:36.300 --> 03:40.220
Which stands for Relativistic Scalar Vector Planum, right?

03:40.260 --> 03:45.080
That's the one. The planum is this smooth, continuous mathematical space where computation

03:45.080 --> 03:46.700
actually executes.

03:46.860 --> 03:47.980
Smooth and continuous.

03:47.980 --> 03:53.460
Right. So when a sphere pops, it follows what they call a curvature-minimizing evolution.

03:53.700 --> 03:57.740
Okay, let's explore that curvature-minimizing evolution, because I know the papers define

03:57.740 --> 03:59.280
it using something called the pop derivative.

03:59.480 --> 03:59.820
Yes.

04:00.220 --> 04:05.640
And if computation is happening in this smooth plenum, the boundary of the sphere, it represents

04:05.640 --> 04:10.480
this sort of tension, right, between the internal evaluated state and the external environment.

04:10.680 --> 04:14.280
Yeah, think of the calculus of variations. Like if you have a wireframe and you dip it in

04:14.280 --> 04:14.900
soapy water.

04:14.900 --> 04:17.520
Oh, right. The soap film naturally forms a shape.

04:17.640 --> 04:23.200
Exactly. It forms a shape that minimizes surface area and surface tension. It's physically

04:23.200 --> 04:25.160
calculating the optimal geometry.

04:25.400 --> 04:27.220
Just by existing. That's so cool.

04:27.280 --> 04:32.620
Right. And the pop derivative works similarly. When the boundary of that computational sphere

04:32.620 --> 04:35.460
collapses, there is an energetic discrepancy.

04:35.700 --> 04:37.140
Like a differential in state.

04:37.340 --> 04:42.880
Yeah, exactly. Between the inside and the outside. So the pop derivative models how that burst

04:42.880 --> 04:45.440
of computational coherence propagates outward.

04:45.700 --> 04:46.880
So it's like ripples in a pond.

04:47.280 --> 04:54.180
Kind of, but it settles into a new topological shape of minimal resistance within the directed

04:54.180 --> 04:55.180
acyclic graph.

04:55.300 --> 04:55.520
Wow.

04:55.520 --> 05:00.040
The computation literally flows down the path of least mathematical resistance.

05:00.400 --> 05:05.560
Wow. Okay, so we have the sphere and the pop. The third and fourth primitives are where this

05:05.560 --> 05:08.560
geometric approach really starts to show its power.

05:08.800 --> 05:09.280
Absolutely.

05:09.680 --> 05:15.520
We've got merge and choice. So merge is parallel composition. Two independent bubbles come together

05:15.520 --> 05:18.300
and fuse, which models concurrent execution.

05:18.420 --> 05:20.120
Right. They just blend into each other.

05:20.120 --> 05:28.060
And then choice introduces stochastic branching, creating a distribution of possibilities within

05:28.060 --> 05:29.240
the geometry itself.

05:29.640 --> 05:35.160
And this is exactly where the RSVP geometry solves a major, major headache in concurrent probability.

05:35.480 --> 05:35.900
How so?

05:36.200 --> 05:39.660
Well, let's look at the independent channels lemma that they presented in the texts.

05:39.840 --> 05:40.060
All right.

05:40.320 --> 05:46.040
If you have a highly complex system with concurrent probabilistic events, say you've got dozens

05:46.040 --> 05:51.360
of branches merging, and each brand has a certain localized probability of failure.

05:51.840 --> 05:57.060
Calculating the aggregate risk of a catastrophic failure across that whole system is usually

05:57.060 --> 05:58.700
incredibly taxing.

05:58.780 --> 06:02.480
Oh, it's a nightmare. You have to account for overlapping states, conditional dependencies,

06:02.760 --> 06:03.200
all of it.

06:03.280 --> 06:08.240
Right. Standard error handling would require you to build these massive, clunky external

06:08.240 --> 06:12.760
architectures just to monitor the failure states of all the individual threads.

06:12.760 --> 06:17.680
But in SpherePop, merge is mathematically treated as a geometric tensor product.

06:17.800 --> 06:18.520
A tensor product.

06:18.720 --> 06:25.080
Yeah. It is a literal fusing of independent probabilistic spaces. So the risk calculation

06:25.080 --> 06:28.540
becomes an inherent property of the geometry itself.

06:28.620 --> 06:30.520
So you don't need the external architecture at all.

06:30.700 --> 06:37.020
Exactly. The independent channels lemma demonstrates that you can evaluate the aggregate disjunctive

06:37.020 --> 06:41.360
risk, which the papers cheerfully term doom, by the way.

06:41.360 --> 06:45.580
Doom. I love that. Very dramatic for a math paper.

06:45.740 --> 06:49.940
Right. But you can calculate doom just by looking at the survival probabilities.

06:50.060 --> 06:50.520
Okay, right.

06:50.620 --> 06:50.760
Yeah.

06:50.880 --> 06:56.320
So rather than trying to add up all the probabilities of failure, which, you know, gets mathematically

06:56.320 --> 06:58.860
tangled if the channels aren't perfectly orthogonal.

06:59.060 --> 06:59.240
Yeah.

06:59.860 --> 07:06.260
You look at the inverse. If you have multiple independent bubbles merging, you calculate the

07:06.260 --> 07:09.780
probability that each individual branch actually survives.

07:09.780 --> 07:10.380
Yes.

07:10.560 --> 07:16.280
And then you take the product of all those independent survival probabilities. That product represents

07:16.280 --> 07:20.820
the chance that the entire merge system successfully navigates the computation.

07:21.080 --> 07:27.240
Exactly. And the probability of a system-wide disjunctive failure doom is simply one minus

07:27.240 --> 07:28.580
that total survival product.

07:28.660 --> 07:29.720
That's incredibly elegant.

07:30.100 --> 07:35.560
It really is. The elegance is that the execution engine doesn't need a heavy, separate error checking

07:35.560 --> 07:41.680
module. By treating the merge as a physical blending of continuous spaces, the risk aggregation

07:41.680 --> 07:44.800
is solved by the physics of the calculus itself.

07:45.060 --> 07:51.120
But wait, let me play devil's advocate here. If we treat computation as these floating merging

07:51.120 --> 07:57.920
bubbles of probabilistic logic, and we remove all those rigid textual constraints, we run into

07:57.920 --> 08:02.820
a major architectural problem, don't we? Like, if an environment variable that's holding a highly

08:02.820 --> 08:08.780
sensitive database key just arbitrarily merges with an untrusted public output stream, we have

08:08.780 --> 08:10.100
a critical security flaw.

08:10.100 --> 08:10.520
Yeah.

08:10.580 --> 08:15.760
So how does this RSVP framework prevent the wrong topological structures from fusing together

08:15.760 --> 08:16.460
in the first place?

08:16.720 --> 08:17.980
That is the big question.

08:18.080 --> 08:22.020
And answering it requires completely rethinking the concept of a type.

08:22.020 --> 08:26.580
Okay. Because standard static typing evaluates a program before it runs.

08:26.720 --> 08:26.820
Yeah.

08:26.860 --> 08:32.000
Right. It categorizes data into strict silos to prevent invalid operations.

08:32.260 --> 08:36.920
Right. But Flyxion introduces a totally different maxim. They say composition before typing.

08:37.160 --> 08:39.280
Opposition before typing. What does that actually mean?

08:39.600 --> 08:45.680
It means they model a program not as a static set of instructions, but as a dynamic unfolding

08:45.680 --> 08:47.720
sequence of historical events.

08:47.940 --> 08:48.600
Like a lineage.

08:48.600 --> 08:56.100
Yes. Exactly. A specific lineage of pops, merges, and collapses. And in this model, a type is

08:56.100 --> 08:59.580
redefined dynamically as what they call a refusal structure.

08:59.720 --> 09:03.600
A refusal structure. Okay. Let me push back on the computational feasibility of that.

09:03.680 --> 09:03.820
Sure.

09:04.320 --> 09:10.280
If a type is no longer a static label, but this dynamic historical boundary, you're implying

09:10.280 --> 09:16.340
that the system has to verify the entire lineage of computational events before allowing a merge.

09:16.480 --> 09:17.360
I see where you're going.

09:17.360 --> 09:22.520
Right. Wouldn't verifying that entire history at runtime be incredibly slow? The DAGs would

09:22.520 --> 09:27.140
just grow exponentially, and the memory overhead just to track the admissibility of a single

09:27.140 --> 09:30.340
variable would, well, it would crush the execution engine.

09:30.420 --> 09:34.420
It would, yeah. That would be true if the system actually had to traverse the entire history graph

09:34.420 --> 09:35.540
for every single operation.

09:35.680 --> 09:36.200
Oh, it doesn't?

09:36.280 --> 09:39.960
No. The RSVP framework utilizes topological compression.

09:39.960 --> 09:47.200
So, the refusal structure doesn't store the literal history. It stores an accumulated cryptographic or

09:47.200 --> 09:49.320
geometric proof of admissibility.

09:49.520 --> 09:50.700
Almost like an ID card.

09:50.840 --> 09:56.800
Yeah. It's a boundary condition. It dictates which future computational histories are mathematically

09:56.800 --> 10:03.340
allowed to continue and which must be blocked. The whole operation is compressed into a singular

10:03.340 --> 10:06.920
geometric signature that can be evaluated in constant time.

10:06.920 --> 10:11.400
Okay. That makes sense. So, it operates almost like a highly sophisticated filter, or, you know,

10:11.440 --> 10:12.540
like a nightclub bouncer.

10:12.700 --> 10:14.160
A bouncer. Yeah, I like that.

10:14.160 --> 10:20.180
All right. It evaluates the geometric signature, the ID of the incoming state. If the signature

10:20.180 --> 10:26.100
is admissible based on the bouncer's rules, the state is allowed to pass through and continue

10:26.100 --> 10:27.240
its execution history.

10:27.360 --> 10:27.580
Yep.

10:27.580 --> 10:30.400
But if the signature fails, the continuation is blocked.

10:30.660 --> 10:30.740
Yeah.

10:30.780 --> 10:32.280
Or, in their terms, refused.

10:32.420 --> 10:38.740
Exactly. And Flyxion draws a very strict operational distinction between a collapse and a refuse.

10:39.140 --> 10:39.660
What's the difference?

10:40.080 --> 10:45.220
Well, a collapse is a successful resolution. A state commits to a value, and the history is

10:45.220 --> 10:48.800
extended. But a refuse is an early termination at the boundary.

10:49.140 --> 10:50.600
Like getting turned away at the door.

10:50.600 --> 10:57.220
Right. Crucially, a type error in SpherePop is not a fatal system crash that halts the

10:57.220 --> 10:58.160
entire DAG.

10:58.520 --> 10:58.980
Oh, wow.

10:59.280 --> 11:04.480
It is a localized protective refusal that just prevents the corrupted state from propagating.

11:04.720 --> 11:08.860
So, it preserves the integrity of the broader computational environment.

11:09.180 --> 11:13.180
But wait, systems can't just block every single error and remain functional, right?

11:13.260 --> 11:13.540
Mm-hmm.

11:13.880 --> 11:19.900
At some point, dynamic environments encounter valid edge cases that need to be actively reconciled.

11:19.900 --> 11:20.580
Yeah, of course.

11:20.800 --> 11:25.240
So, if a type is just a boundary condition, how does the system recover from a state that is

11:25.240 --> 11:29.400
technically valid, but geometrically misaligned with the next operation?

11:29.840 --> 11:35.200
So, the Flyxion researchers actually integrate the calculus of constructions directly into the

11:35.200 --> 11:37.700
SpherePop framework to address exactly this.

11:37.960 --> 11:38.580
Oh, interesting.

11:38.680 --> 11:42.300
Yeah. They utilize dependent types as active repair operators.

11:42.580 --> 11:47.200
Dependent types. Usually, those defined types that depend on specific values calculated

11:47.200 --> 11:48.120
at runtime, right?

11:48.120 --> 11:53.660
Normally, yes. But in a geometric context, they act as active transitional mechanisms.

11:53.940 --> 11:54.280
How so?

11:54.480 --> 11:59.540
The papers detail the pi-type, the dependent function, as a universal repair operator.

11:59.680 --> 12:01.020
Universal repair operator.

12:01.160 --> 12:06.180
Yeah. Its function isn't merely to categorize data. It is literally a procedure that takes

12:06.180 --> 12:08.760
any admitted history and safely extends it.

12:08.800 --> 12:09.680
So, it fixes the shape.

12:09.680 --> 12:15.960
Exactly. If a state reaches a boundary and requires reconciliation, the pi-type actively

12:15.960 --> 12:21.120
transforms the topological shape of the state to ensure it aligns with the requirements of

12:21.120 --> 12:22.360
the next phase of the deck.

12:22.440 --> 12:24.860
Okay. That's the pi-type. And what about the sigma-type?

12:25.000 --> 12:29.640
The sigma-type, the dependent sum, that operates as what they call a witnessed repair.

12:29.900 --> 12:30.520
Witness repair.

12:30.520 --> 12:37.040
Yeah. It pairs an admitted state, the witness, with the specific mathematical evidence required

12:37.040 --> 12:39.840
to prove its admissibility to the next boundary.

12:40.060 --> 12:41.420
Oh, so it's like a chaperone.

12:41.920 --> 12:46.780
Sort of. It bundles the data and the proof of its valid history into a single geometric

12:46.780 --> 12:50.760
construct, which allows it to seamlessly pass through the refusal structure.

12:50.760 --> 12:56.920
That is fascinating, because the execution engine is only concerned with evaluating these

12:56.920 --> 12:58.540
geometric boundary conditions, right?

12:58.540 --> 12:58.700
Yeah.

12:58.900 --> 13:02.440
Just admitting or refusing states based on their topological signatures.

13:02.860 --> 13:03.140
Right.

13:03.440 --> 13:07.780
So, it is entirely blind to the semantic meaning of the logic it's actually processing.

13:08.020 --> 13:10.740
Completely blind. The engine only cares about readiness.

13:11.120 --> 13:17.620
Readiness, meaning the evaluation of a node in the DAG the exact moment its required inputs

13:17.620 --> 13:20.340
arrive and are admitted by the refusal structure.

13:20.340 --> 13:20.820
Yes.

13:21.080 --> 13:26.480
This turns the underlying execution environment into, like, pure infrastructure.

13:26.680 --> 13:27.540
It really does.

13:27.800 --> 13:31.280
Imagine a massive network of pipes and valves, you know.

13:32.440 --> 13:35.640
The plumbing in the city doesn't care what kind of fluid you pump through it.

13:35.900 --> 13:39.000
It only checks if the valve is open and if there's pressure in the line.

13:39.120 --> 13:40.040
That's a great analogy.

13:40.280 --> 13:40.540
Thanks.

13:41.060 --> 13:46.620
But because of this architectural blindness, Boolean logic, you know, the binary true-false

13:46.620 --> 13:50.240
foundation of all traditional computing, it isn't structurally privileged.

13:50.340 --> 13:51.260
It's an SPC, is it?

13:51.560 --> 13:52.220
Not at all.

13:52.280 --> 13:56.460
It is just one specific operator library that you happen to load into the plumbing.

13:56.680 --> 14:02.600
Which means you can seamlessly swap the Boolean library for a fuzzy logic library without

14:02.600 --> 14:04.320
altering the underlying DAG.

14:04.320 --> 14:04.800
Exactly.

14:04.800 --> 14:08.860
And in fuzzy logic, truth values aren't discrete binary states.

14:09.040 --> 14:11.320
They're continuous values between zero and one.

14:11.580 --> 14:13.280
They represent degrees of admissibility.

14:14.100 --> 14:20.040
So the geometric plumbing handles a continuous flow of probabilistic values just as easily

14:20.040 --> 14:23.660
as it handles discrete packages of binary data.

14:23.660 --> 14:27.620
Which significantly alters how we handle control flow, doesn't it?

14:27.720 --> 14:30.960
I mean, a standard conditional if-false statement is a hard fork.

14:31.520 --> 14:35.680
The execution path has to choose one route and discard the other completely.

14:36.260 --> 14:41.400
But in SpherePop, that if-false construct is reduced to a standard select operator.

14:42.060 --> 14:47.160
And under a fuzzy logic paradigm, the system isn't actually forced into a binary choice.

14:47.260 --> 14:47.780
It's not.

14:47.780 --> 14:51.340
No, it can engage in what Flyxion calls delayed commitment.

14:51.600 --> 14:52.380
Delayed commitment.

14:52.680 --> 14:58.280
Okay, so that implies the execution graph can explore multiple concurrent paths simultaneously

14:58.280 --> 15:00.480
without actually collapsing the state.

15:00.660 --> 15:01.080
Precisely.

15:01.140 --> 15:05.020
If the DAG encounters a conditional fork, it just distributes the probabilistic weight across

15:05.020 --> 15:05.700
both branches.

15:05.920 --> 15:06.240
Yes.

15:06.840 --> 15:11.640
The computational history accumulates evidence along parallel routes, maintaining this sort

15:11.640 --> 15:13.820
of superposition of potential outcomes.

15:13.980 --> 15:14.560
Until what?

15:14.560 --> 15:19.160
Until an observer node or a strict boundary condition forces a definitive collapse.

15:19.520 --> 15:19.840
Wow.

15:20.580 --> 15:26.400
And the elimination of rigid control flow in favor of this continuous geometric evaluation,

15:26.720 --> 15:30.280
that also redefines the whole concept of compilation, doesn't it?

15:30.320 --> 15:30.900
Oh, absolutely.

15:30.900 --> 15:34.480
Standard compilation translates high-level syntax into machine code.

15:34.960 --> 15:38.760
But in SBC, compilation is purely graph rewriting.

15:39.000 --> 15:39.620
Graph rewriting.

15:39.740 --> 15:39.900
Yeah.

15:40.080 --> 15:44.500
Optimizing a program involves literally physically reshaping the topological

15:44.500 --> 15:49.720
layout of the DAG to reduce the latency of the flow, all while preserving the mathematical

15:49.720 --> 15:51.060
integrity of the history.

15:51.060 --> 15:54.860
And that graph rewriting applies directly to machine learning, too, doesn't it?

15:55.100 --> 16:00.160
Because backpropagation, the mechanism that allows neural networks to update their weights,

16:00.640 --> 16:04.260
that's usually treated as a highly specialized architectural requirement.

16:04.260 --> 16:09.720
Right, but under the Flyxion paradigm, backpropagation is just a backward traversal of the exact same

16:09.720 --> 16:10.740
composition graph.

16:10.860 --> 16:11.500
It's the same graph.

16:11.500 --> 16:12.480
The exact same graph.

16:12.580 --> 16:16.780
Instead of pushing state forward using logic operators, the engine just pushes gradients

16:16.780 --> 16:18.800
backward using derivative operators.

16:18.940 --> 16:19.820
That is wild.

16:20.100 --> 16:22.160
The geometric substrate doesn't change at all.

16:22.760 --> 16:28.400
Classical deterministic logic, fuzzy probability, and neural network training all run on the identical

16:28.400 --> 16:29.920
topological infrastructure.

16:29.920 --> 16:36.200
Okay, so if this framework unifies discrete logic, continuous probability, and machine

16:36.200 --> 16:42.340
learning all within a single geometric space, we have to consider what happens when highly

16:42.340 --> 16:46.200
autonomous intelligent agents operate within that same space.

16:46.460 --> 16:47.260
Yes, we do.

16:47.480 --> 16:52.680
Because this is where the mathematical mechanics of SpherePop intersect with the theoretical alignment

16:52.680 --> 16:54.660
of artificial general intelligence, right?

16:54.780 --> 16:57.100
This is where the papers get truly profound.

16:57.100 --> 17:02.940
The Flyxion group dedicates a substantial portion of their research to applying the relativistic

17:02.940 --> 17:05.360
scalar vector plenum to AI safety.

17:05.500 --> 17:08.120
And they actively critique the dominant paradigm, right?

17:08.120 --> 17:08.300
Yeah.

17:08.400 --> 17:10.020
Which they call the paradox of precaution.

17:10.200 --> 17:10.440
Right.

17:10.720 --> 17:14.520
Because the current consensus in AI safety heavily relies on containment.

17:14.840 --> 17:15.900
Like boxing the AI.

17:16.020 --> 17:16.420
Exactly.

17:16.900 --> 17:21.860
The objective is to build an unbreachable box around the AGI, restricting its outputs and

17:21.860 --> 17:25.180
rigidly defining its alignment parameters before it ever gets deployed.

17:25.180 --> 17:25.660
